#{[17592186446487 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446473 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446487 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83437" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446487 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446487 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)={x}^{2}+y$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 1\\}.$ You are given that the function has no critical points." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446487 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "a2f4f1da-3ab7-5268-ad0e-cfd1ed45e614" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446487 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ that lie inside the region $D.$ \n\nSince f has no critical points in D , the list of candidates for the extrema won't contain critical points.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding circle $4{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}=1.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}{2},\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡t}{4}+\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{′} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{2}+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡t(2-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $|\\operatorname{sin}⁡t|\\le 1$, this equation gives the solutions $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}\\in (0,2\\pi ).$\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval,  $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can possibly give rise to extreme values are $t=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\,$ and $\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}.$ Evaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain candidate points: \n\n$$\n(\\frac{1}{2},0),\\,(0,1),\\,(0,-1)\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values.\n\nLet's compare the values of $f(x,y)$ evaluated at each of the points mentioned above. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(\\frac{1}{2},0) & ={(\\frac{1}{2})}^{2}+0 \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4} \\\\ f(0,1) & ={0}^{2}+1 \\\\  & =1 \\\\ f(0,-1) & ={0}^{2}+(-1) \\\\  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the point $(0,-1),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the point $(0,1).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446488 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446467 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446488 #edb/ref 1069 "q-86706" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446488 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446488 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/9c/9ce1b06ac9fc3caeed0a1f7866511be42b4c127a0bdddfd40b74e211ed24e02a.png)\n\nFind the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=2x{y}^{2}$ on the closed region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x\\ge 0,\\,y\\ge 0,\\,{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 4\\},$ shown above." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446488 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e240afca-8d54-5fc1-a704-b3948ecfdb13" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446488 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$\n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo determine the critical points, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}2{y}^{2},\\,4xy\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2} &=0 \\\\ 4xy &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=R \\\\ y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us the entire segment of solutions $\\{(x,0)\\,:\\,x\\in [0,2]\\}.$ Since all these points do not belong to the interior of $D,$ we do not yet include them in the list of candidates for extrema (they lie on the boundary of $D,$ so we will consider them in the next step).\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize each part of the boundary:\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨t,0⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,0) \\\\  & =2(t)(0)^{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have local extremum points on the open interval $t\\in (0,2).$\n\n- The line connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,2)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\nr=⟨0,t⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on this boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(0,t) \\\\  & =2(0)(t)^{2} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that $F(t)$ is constant. So, $F(t)$ does not have local extremum points on the open interval $t\\in (0,2).$\n\n- The quarter-circle connecting $(0,2)$ and $(2,0)$ can be parametrized as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =⟨t,\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}⟩,\\,t\\in (0,2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(t,\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}}) \\\\  & =2(t)(\\sqrt{4-{t}^{2}})^{2} \\\\  & =8t-2{t}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (8t-2{t}^{3})^{′} & =0 \\\\ 8-6{t}^{2} & =0 \\\\ 2(4-3{t}^{2}) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives one solution, $t=\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},$ inside the interval $t\\in (0,2).$\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain one  candidate point, $(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}).$\n\nAlso, we have to include the corner points of the region as candidates for extrema. So, in total we obtain four candidates:\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(2,0),\\,(0,2),\\,(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(2,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $0$ | $0$ | $\\frac{32\\sqrt{3}}{9}$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the points on the line segment connecting $(0,0)$ and $(2,0),$ and on the line segment connecting $(0,0)$ and $(0,2),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $\\frac{32\\sqrt{3}}{9}$ at the point $(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{2\\sqrt{6}}{3}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186446489 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186446472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446489 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83438" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446489 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446489 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=3xy$ on the closed elliptical region $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}\\le 2\\}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446489 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e78c82da-b48f-5dab-aeb6-fcde924129f1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186446489 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$  \n\nTo find the extreme values, we follow three steps.\n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points of $f$ inside the region. \n\nTo do that, we solve ${f}^{′}=0.$ Computing the derivative gives\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}3y,\\,3x\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nThe derivative exists at each point inside $D,$ and it equals zero when\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}3y &=0 \\\\ 3x &=0\\end{aligned}\\right.\\,⟹\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}x &=0 \\\\ y &=0.\\end{aligned}\\right.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives us a single point $(0,0)\\in D,$ which we include in the list of candidates for the extrema.\n\n**Step 2.**  Find the points on the boundary of $D$ that could give rise to extreme values. \n\nTo find the boundary points that could give rise to extreme values, we parametrize the bounding ellipse ${x}^{2}+9{y}^{2}=2.$ This gives\n\n$$\nr=⟨\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t⟩,\\,t\\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nThe values of $f$ on the boundary are given by the function\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}F(t) & =f(x(t),y(t)) \\\\  & =f(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =3(\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡t) \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cos}⁡t\\operatorname{sin}⁡t \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nTo find the critical points of $F(t)$ on the open interval $t\\in (0,2\\pi ),$ we solve the following equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(t) & =0 \\\\ (\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2t))^{′} & =0 \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0 \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(2t) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe final equation gives four solutions, namely\n\n$$\nt=\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}\\in (0,2\\pi ).\n$$\n\nWe also have to check the endpoint of the interval $t=0.$ Therefore, the only values of $t$ that can give rise to extreme values are \n\n$$\nt=0,\\,\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{3\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{5\\pi }{4},\\,\\frac{7\\pi }{4}.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the corresponding values of $x$ and $y$ on the boundary, we obtain five candidate points:\n\n$$\n(\\sqrt{2},0),\\,(1,\\frac{1}{3}),\\,(-1,\\frac{1}{3}),\\,(-1,-\\frac{1}{3}),\\,(1,-\\frac{1}{3})\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following: \n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(\\sqrt{2},0)$ | $(1,\\frac{1}{3})$ | $(-1,\\frac{1}{3})$ | $(-1,-\\frac{1}{3})$ | $(1,-\\frac{1}{3})$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $0$ | $0$ | $1$ | $-1$ | $1$ | $-1$ |\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $-1$ at the points $(-1,\\frac{1}{3})$ and $(1,-\\frac{1}{3}),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $1$ at the points $(1,\\frac{1}{3})$ and $(-1,-\\frac{1}{3}).$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186483555 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186484299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483555 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186484306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483555 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186484315 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483555 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186484340 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483555 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186484346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483555 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483555 #edb/ref 1045 "Calculating Global Extrema of Multivariable Functions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483555 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186484306 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483555 #edb/ref 1048 #edb/ref 17592186046660 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483555 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483555 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "014bb5c2-0eb1-526d-946d-25c9eb45386c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483667 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483667 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483667 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "37223220-8ce2-5aaf-b13b-e51831642442" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483675 #edb/ref 1080 "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=16$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483675 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483675 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3a98d925-ab88-52f8-884f-7e6b8120f7cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483679 #edb/ref 1080 "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483679 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483679 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3d039668-18b1-5d4a-9869-1e01eaef1bf8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483690 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483690 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483690 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "4141a628-787e-59cd-9b3b-4d9ed6c8303b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483691 #edb/ref 1080 "0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483691 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483691 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "42c9840a-d25c-56c5-a13d-22ba7d99829d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483729 #edb/ref 1080 "${f}_{min}=1$ and ${f}_{max}=4$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483729 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483729 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "589e8125-b8d8-5115-be4b-3627bde6a5ff" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483870 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483870 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186483870 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "91ce1e4d-9d5f-507c-b1a8-339a62262fd9" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484038 #edb/ref 1080 "${f}_{min}=-2$ and ${f}_{max}=0$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484038 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484038 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "edc7a1cd-95f8-56b1-b089-7c0beaf8c25d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484057 #edb/ref 1080 "${f}_{min}=0$ and ${f}_{max}=16$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484057 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484057 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f3c7bece-f4b8-5686-919a-a5cb331f67ba" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484080 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484080 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484080 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fde67504-6547-54d3-949e-2a67417d8d96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484125 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484125 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483690 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484125 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484125 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483870 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484125 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186484080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484125 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484125 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484125 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186483691 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484125 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "585700db-4a4c-5c97-98d7-30b257ff65a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484136 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483675 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484136 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483679 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484136 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186483729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484136 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186484038 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484136 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186484057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484136 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484136 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484136 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186484057 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484136 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "750f6f9e-b6e0-5035-8e03-025f023dadec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484220 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186484136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484220 #edb/ref 1069 "q-37146" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484220 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484220 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the global extrema of the function $f(x,y)=(x-1)^{2}+{y}^{2}$ on the closed region  $D=\\{(x,y):{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}\\le 9\\}.$ You are given that $f$ has a critical point at  $(1,0)$ and on the boundary of the region the points that could give rise to extreme values are  $(-3,0)$, and $(3,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484220 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "50860c27-301d-5231-9268-fbee285d7c09" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484220 #edb/ref 1074 "Since $f(x,y)$ is continuous on the closed, bounded region $D,$ the function attains a global maximum and minimum on $D.$ \n\n**Step 1.** Find the critical points inside the region $D.$ \n\nTo determine the critical points, let's first sketch the region:\n\n![](images/d7/d7864a36585a145e009846b23142372caeff3a68f17c9d1f7d1cf17e642f9543.png)\n\nSince the critical point $(1,0)$ lies inside $D,$ we include that point in the list of candidates for the extrema. \n\n**Step 2.** Find the points on the boundary that could give rise to extreme values.\n\nIn this case, we are *given* the points on the boundary of our region that could potentially be global extrema for $f.$ These are\n\n$$\n(3,0),\\,(-3,0).\n$$\n\n**Step 3.** Evaluate $f(x,y)$ at each of the candidate points, and compare their values. \n\nEvaluating $f(x,y)$ at each of the points mentioned above, we get the following:  \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(1,0) & =(1-1)^{2}+(0)^{2} \\\\  & =0 \\\\ f(3,0) & =(2)^{2}+(0)^{2} \\\\  & =4 \\\\ f(-3,0) & =(-4)^{2}+(0)^{2} \\\\  & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, on the closed region $D,$ we have that \n\n- $f$ attains the global minimum of $0$ at the point $(1,0),$ and\n\n- $f$ attains the global maximum of $16$ at the point $(-3,0).$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484299 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186484346 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186484299 #e